{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-review-c4.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 35 reviewed the crash lead's first check and kept the inference boundary explicit.\\n\\nState on entry. The new claim C4-noop-commit-without-bookkeeping has one artifact, 986da6b1d390428da40aa49703011db0 (the check submitted last turn), with outcome violated and oracle correspondence unreviewed. Its harness creates the record with the real handlePreAccept for (Replica 0, Instance 0) at ballot 3 with one PUT command and then delivers a well-formed single-NONE Commit at ballot 4 to the real handleCommit, wrapping only the delivery in recover().\\n\\nReview judgement (checker_correspondence, no_issue_found). Requirement versus predicate: the claim is that a legal NOOP commit for an instance the replica owns must be processed without dereferencing absent bookkeeping and without terminating the replica, and the predicate asserts panicked == false on the commit-result event, which the run reports true together with the nil-pointer panic value and no_effect true — so the assertion is evaluated on printed fields. Entry and message legality: both messages are the protocol's own for a recovery-led NOOP (the owner receives a PreAccept for its row from a recovering leader, then the Commit the recoverer broadcasts after restarting phase 1), which is legal for a replica that no longer holds the instance in memory. Oracle and wrapper: the recover() covers only the delivery and is what makes the panic observable, not what creates it, which the printed panic value confirms. Identity: the prerequisite record event and the result event share instance 0.0. Inference boundary: the run loop dispatches handlers directly in its select with no recover, so a handler panic propagates and ends the process — that step is a source-based inference and is recorded as a limitation rather than as an observed process exit, since the harness does not drive the loop. Alternatives and reservations are in the rationale and limitations: the reading that such a record can never belong to the owner is contradicted by the message-created record; the history that would put a restarted owner in that state is not constructed; and the client-side consequence of the same NOOP recovery remains a separate question.\\n\\nState. Audit spec version 6; the three earlier confirmed claims (C1's in-scope properties, C2's stable-store record, C3's applied-state effect) are untouched; the accept-status candidate stays paused; the client-completion candidate remains active with its unknowns, and the scope-issue bookkeeping debt on the superseded C1 artifact stays visible. No requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened.\"}"}}
